          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"Goneril directly presents her love for Lear as true and intensifies it through an extended comparison. Whatever her sincerity, the expression has clear avowal/reporting force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"The expression avows present love but does not itself promise future conduct, assume a new obligation, or specify a relational undertaking. Lear has made the declaration relevant to his distribution of property, but that surrounding transaction does not turn Goneril's words into a promise.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"The declaration is part of a composed, rhetorically elaborate answer to Lear's formal demand, rather than a reflex-like affective outburst. Its intensity does not by itself establish expressive-discharge force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"A love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Beyond all manner of so much I love you.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"No additional core force beyond the love avowal is adequately supported. The speech may also flatter or compete for Lear's bounty, but the focal expression itself remains fundamentally an assertion of love rather than a distinct act such as requesting, commanding, or promising.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"That we our largest bounty may extend\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to say which loves him most while announcing that his largest bounty will follow the greatest claimed love. Goneril, speaking first, answers with an extravagant declaration that she loves her father beyond things such as sight, liberty, and life.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"mixed\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression contains no identifiable undertaking by Goneril. Lear responds to her declaration by granting territory to her and Albany, but this is a reward for the avowal in the staged love contest, not uptake of a promise. The supplied passage does not settle Goneril's sincerity; her explicit profession supports affection, while the competitive, materially incentivized setting and extreme rhetoric make the actual state uncertain.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"This passage is recognisable as the opening love-test scene from William Shakespeare's King Lear. The assessment relies only on the supplied text rather than later plot events.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied scene establishes the public and materially consequential setting but does not independently determine whether Goneril genuinely feels the love she avows. Later conduct or revelations outside the supplied passage have not been used.\"}"
